(()=>{const MVX=[[0,"nerves",11],[1,"nerves",13],[2,"nerves",6],[3,"nerves",7],[5,"muscles",3]],bx=B["bones~x"],mv=MVX.map(([i,k,c])=>[bx[i],k,c]);mv.forEach(([q,k,c])=>{q.l=k;q.c=c;(B[k+"~x"]=B[k+"~x"]||[]).push(q)});B["bones~x"]=bx.filter(q=>!mv.some(m=>m[0]===q)).concat([["You compare scapular position with the arms relaxed by the side. The right inferior angle is level with about T7, but the left sits at about T9. What is the most reasonable interpretation?", ["The left scapula sits lower than the right, so compare it with movement and posture", "The left scapula is normal, because the inferior angle always lies at T9", "The right scapula must be fractured, because it sits higher than expected", "Vertebral levels cannot be used at all, so the finding should be ignored"], 0, "T7 is a rough guide that varies between people, so the most useful thing is comparing sides. A two-level difference suggests the left scapula sits lower, which is worth relating to posture and movement. One level alone does not prove a fracture or a fixed normal value.", 5], ["A student says the bony tip they can feel on the superior aspect of the shoulder is the coracoid process. Which palpation finding would show it is actually the acromion?", ["It is continuous with the spine of the scapula when traced medially", "It lies inferior to the lateral clavicle on the anterior shoulder", "It sits at the level of the inferior angle of the scapula", "It can only be felt from the anterior aspect, deep to soft tissue"], 0, "The spine of the scapula continues laterally as the acromion, so tracing it confirms the landmark. The coracoid lies anteriorly, inferior to the lateral clavicle, and is felt from the front.", 2], ["After a fall onto the point of the shoulder, a patient has a tender step at the lateral end of the clavicle. Which two bones meet at this point?", ["Clavicle and acromion", "Clavicle and coracoid process", "Clavicle and sternum", "Acromion and head of the humerus"], 0, "The lateral end of the clavicle meets the acromion at the acromioclavicular (AC) joint, a common site of injury after a fall onto the point of the shoulder. The sternum is at the medial end.", 2], ["Compared with the hip, why does the glenohumeral joint dislocate more easily?", ["The shallow glenoid fossa covers only part of the large humeral head", "The humeral head is smaller than the glenoid fossa", "The glenoid fossa faces inferiorly, so gravity pulls the head out", "The clavicle holds the humerus rigidly, so forces cannot be absorbed"], 0, "The glenoid fossa is shallow and covers only part of the much larger humeral head. This gives a large range of movement but little bony stability. The hip socket is deep and surrounds most of the femoral head.", 4]].map(a=>({q:a[0],o:a[1],a:a[2],e:a[3],c:a[4],l:"bones"})))})();
